      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kerman began as a Southern Pacific Railroad watering station in 1891, originally named Collis after railroad president Collis Potter Huntington.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofkerman.gov/256/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1892, the infamous Sontag and Evans gang robbed a Southern Pacific passenger train at Collis, one of the last train robberies in the U.S.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofkerman.gov/256/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name changed to Kerman in 1906, a blend of the first three letters of investors Kerckhoff and Jacob Mansar.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofkerman.gov/256/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Kerman sits at the crossroads of State Routes 180 and 145, 15 miles west of Fresno, at 220 feet elevation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerman%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The original pump and tank caretaker, who kept water for thirsty engines, resigned after months of solitude, never meeting anyone else.",
